## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical CKNE-to-quotas mapping, token-based fairness, and SLA guarantees in a real-world multi-tenant dispatch scenario.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-quotas mapping
- Token-bucket with a global minute cap
- Minute-by-minute reallocation and carryover
- SLA baseline with priority to high-CKNE tenants

## Code Example
```javascript
function allocateQuotas(totalCap, tenants) {
  const sumCKNE = tenants.reduce((s,t)=>s+t.ckne,0) || 1;
  return tenants.map(t => {
    const q = Math.floor(totalCap * (t.ckne / sumCKNE));
    return {tenant: t.tenant, quota: Math.max(1, q)};
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle new tenants joining or CKNE updates during a minute?
- What telemetry would you collect to verify fairness and SLA adherence?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world BI workloads multiplex dozens to thousands of tenants; CKNE-driven concurrency and memory control is critical for SLOs and fair sharing.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-resource mapping: concurrency, memory budget, spill/IO throttling
- Per-tenant quotas and dynamic reallocation cadence
- Fairness guarantees and explicit preemption rules
- Telemetry hooks and feedback for regret-free adjustment

## Code Example
```javascript
function allocate(tenants){
  // normalize CKNE 0-1 to weight, compute quotas
  const total = tenants.reduce((a,t)=>a+t.ckne,0);
  tenants.forEach(t=>{ t.alloc = t.ckne/total; });
  return tenants;
}
```

